    },
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I think D, propane, because it is non-polar, but I am not sure about the others."
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "The learner has correctly identified propane as non-polar. Confirm and briefly compare the intermolecular forces in the options, then state the answer and the inverse relationship needed for this question.",
      "content": "Yes, propane is non-polar, so its molecules experience only weak London dispersion forces. The other options have stronger attractions: ethanal has dipole-dipole forces, while ethanol and methanoic acid can form hydrogen bonds. Weaker attractions let molecules escape more easily, giving propane the highest vapour pressure.\n\n**Final answer: D — $\\text{CH}_3\\text{CH}_2\\text{CH}_3$**\n\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Formula:** $P_{\\text{vap}} \\propto \\frac{1}{F_{\\text{IMF}}}$\n* **What the letters mean:**\n  * $P_{\\text{vap}}$: vapour pressure—the pressure from molecules that have evaporated.\n  * $F_{\\text{IMF}}$: the strength of the intermolecular forces holding the molecules together.\n* **Why we use it:** Vapour pressure and intermolecular-force strength are inversely related. Weaker forces mean more molecules evaporate, so propane has the highest vapour pressure.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** For this type of multiple-choice question, identify the substance with the weakest intermolecular forces; do not confuse stronger bonding between molecules with higher vapour pressure."
    }
  ],
  "meta": {
    "strategy_used": "step_walkthrough",
    "final_answer": "D",
