    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "The learner correctly notices that ethane and ethene contain carbon and hydrogen, but is treating “hydrocarbon” as the only condition. The trap is that ethane is saturated: it has only single bonds between carbon atoms. Contrast the two requirements, then give the answer and a concise classification rule.",
      "content": "You’ve spotted why ethane is a hydrocarbon, but there’s a second condition: an **unsaturated** hydrocarbon must also have a carbon–carbon double or triple bond. Ethane has only single bonds, so option A does not qualify.\n\n**Final answer: B — ethene and ethyne.** Ethene has a C=C double bond, and ethyne has a C≡C triple bond. Options C and D include oxygen-containing substances, so those substances are not hydrocarbons.\n\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Formula:** \\(\\text{Unsaturated hydrocarbon} \\iff \\text{only C and H} \\land (\\mathrm{C=C}\\text{ or }\\mathrm{C\\equiv C})\\)\n* **What the letters mean:**\n  * **C and H:** carbon and hydrogen; a hydrocarbon contains only these elements.\n  * **C=C or C≡C:** a double or triple bond between carbon atoms; either makes the hydrocarbon unsaturated.\n* **Why we use it:** Check both conditions. Having only carbon and hydrogen makes a substance a hydrocarbon, but it is unsaturated only if it has a carbon–carbon double or triple bond. That is why ethane is excluded, while ethene and ethyne fit.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** Don’t stop after checking that a compound contains carbon and hydrogen. Check its carbon–carbon bonds too: single bonds only means saturated; a double or triple bond means unsaturated."
