      "role": "user",
      "content": "Both reactions, I think. So the mixture reaches equilibrium faster, but the catalyst doesn't change the balance between SO₂, O₂ and SO₃. The yield should stay the same."
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "The learner has corrected the misconception: a catalyst speeds up both directions and does not shift the equilibrium position. Confirm the reasoning, map the analogy to the reaction, and state option C with an exam-ready explanation. A simple illustrative check shows equal rate factors preserve their ratio. [TOOL CHECK: calculator(expression=\"2 / 2\") -> 1]",
      "content": "Yes: the catalyst speeds up both directions, so equilibrium is reached sooner, but the equilibrium mixture—and therefore the yield of SO₃—stays the same. In the doorway analogy, people pass through faster in both directions, but the doorway itself does not change the balance between the rooms.\n\n**Final answer: C — Yield remains the same; reaction rate increases.**\n\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Formula:** $v_{f,\\text{cat}} > v_{f,\\text{uncat}}$ and $v_{r,\\text{cat}} > v_{r,\\text{uncat}}$; at constant temperature, $K_c$ remains unchanged.\n* **What the letters mean:**\n  * $v_f$: the speed of the forward reaction, forming SO₃.\n  * $v_r$: the speed of the reverse reaction, forming SO₂ and O₂.\n  * $K_c$: the equilibrium constant, which describes the balance of substances at equilibrium.\n* **Why we use it:** A catalyst provides an alternative pathway with lower activation energy. This makes both the forward and reverse reactions faster. Because it speeds them up without changing the equilibrium position, the mixture reaches equilibrium sooner but the yield remains unchanged.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** Remember that a catalyst changes the **rate**, not the **position of equilibrium** or the equilibrium yield. It speeds up both the forward and reverse reactions."
    }
  ],
  "meta": {
