      "content": "HCl does have a higher $[\\text{H}_3\\text{O}^+]$, but a higher hydronium concentration means a **lower** pH. HCl ionises completely, while acetic acid only partially ionises, so the correct choice is **B**.\n\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Formula:** $\\text{pH}=-\\log_{10}[\\text{H}_3\\text{O}^+]$\n* **What the letters mean:**\n  * $\\text{pH}$: how acidic the solution is; a lower pH means a more acidic solution.\n  * $[\\text{H}_3\\text{O}^+]$: the hydronium ion concentration in the solution.\n* **Why we use it:** The negative sign means that when hydronium concentration increases, pH decreases. Since HCl is a strong acid and acetic acid is weak, HCl produces more hydronium at equal concentrations.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** Strong versus weak acids can have different hydronium concentrations at the same acid concentration. Remember the inverse relationship: higher $[\\text{H}_3\\text{O}^+]$ means lower pH."
